{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NXXCsnPTgtMNj2NqG8UwTE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T205155\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: borderline\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T21:33:24.355193+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: OntoCoDec\n9\t\n10\t**Paper:** OntoCoDec: Near-Complete Type-Valid Information Extraction via Compiled Ontology Constraints\n11\t**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n12\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n13\t**Date:** 2026-05-14\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Venue Scoring Basis\n18\t\n19\tEMNLP 2026 uses ACL Rolling Review (ARR). Three scoring dimensions (1–5 scale): **Soundness** (technical rigor), **Excitement** (novelty/impact), **Overall Assessment** (composite recommendation). Criteria sourced from [ARR Review Form](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform) and [ARR Reviewer Guidelines](http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines).\n20\t\n21\t---\n22\t\n23\t## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n24\t\n25\t### Reviewer 1 (R1) — Methods & Experiments\n26\t\n27\t**Scores:** Soundness=4, Excitement=3, Overall=3, Confidence=4\n28\t\n29\t**Strengths:**\n30\t- **Thorough ablation design.** The 4-way ablation (unconstrained / grammar-only / semantic-only / full S1) on both datasets cleanly isolates each layer's contribution. The enabling dependency claim is well-supported: semantic-only achieves only 24% DR reduction on SciERC vs. 94% for the full system, with a convincing mechanistic explanation (OOV relation names block domain/range lookup).\n31\t- **Honest treatment of negative results.** CF trade-off (9.90% SciERC, 12.02% Llama-DocRED), grammar-only DR *increase* on DocRED (18.46→26.17%), and S2 null result are all reported transparently with structural explanations.\n32\t- **Statistical rigor.** 10,000-iteration bootstrap with CIs, McNemar tests for prompt comparison, per-dataset significance reporting (SciERC p=0.58 neutral, DocRED p<0.001 significant) rather than cherry-picking.\n33\t- **Cross-model 2×2 validation** with genuine dual-factor insight (DR reduction mirrors RV baseline ordering).\n34\t- **Post-hoc baseline** framed as multi-dimensional comparison (recall, coverage, TP loss) rather than F1 contest. The 127 lost TPs on DocRED is compelling.\n35\t- **Constraint tightness analysis** provides a clean causal chain: tightness → fallback rate → residual DR.\n36\t\n37\t**Weaknesses:**\n38\t- **2×2 scope narrow for generality of claims.** Two 8B-class models, two datasets, zero-shot only. No larger model (e.g., 70B), no fine-tuned model. The practical motivation for constrained decoding is arguably stronger in the fine-tuned regime where F1 is meaningful.\n39\t- **Very low absolute F1 (~8% R-F1) raises ecological validity questions.** Constraint enforcement on a distribution ~92% wrong may exhibit different dynamics than on a higher-accuracy regime. The tightness-fallback-DR relationship may not hold when the base model is more accurate.\n40\t- **CF trade-off substantial and severity distinction unvalidated.** DR violations framed as \"hard\" and CF as \"soft,\" but no downstream task evaluation confirms this. The dominant pattern (Generic↔OtherScientificTerm) is attributed to type system ambiguity — this risks dismissing a method limitation as a dataset artifact.\n41\t- **Decidability taxonomy limited novelty.** The local/prefix-decidable/non-local distinction is well-known in constraint satisfaction and compiler literature. Presented as a contribution but reads as design rationale.\n42\t- **DocRED 10.21% residual DR (Qwen) strains \"near-complete\" title.** One of four matrix cells retains >10% violations.\n43\t- **No comparison with fine-tuned or training-based methods.** Even a few-shot or LoRA baseline would contextualize the practical value.\n44\t- **Semantic-only ET increase on DocRED (2.48→4.22%) concerning.** Partial constraint application making a different violation category worse is a negative interaction effect that warrants deeper discussion.\n45\t\n46\t**Key Questions:**\n47\t1. Is the fallback-rate / residual-DR near-exact match (DocRED ~10.2% each) a mathematical tautology or an empirical finding?\n48\t2. Would CF persist with a fine-tuned model producing more accurate types?\n49\t3. What happens when first-token masking uniqueness assumption fails (different tokenizer, larger type vocabulary)?\n50\t4. Parse failure increase for Llama-DocRED (68→91): can increasing max_new_tokens eliminate this?\n51\t5. Grammar-only R-F1 on DocRED (8.31) > Full S1 (8.23) — does the semantic layer introduce systematic negative F1 pressure?\n52\t\n53\t---\n54\t\n55\t### Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty & Related Work\n56\t\n57\t**Scores:** Soundness=4, Excitement=2, Overall=3, Confidence=4\n58\t\n59\t**Strengths:**\n60\t- **Violation landscape study is a genuine empirical contribution.** No prior work has systematically characterized per-relation ontology constraint violation distributions in LLM-based IE. The tightness-violation correlation (SciERC 7 relations at 15.4% DR ≈ DocRED 96 relations at 18.5% DR) is non-obvious. The Evaluate-for regression (+5.8pp under schema prompting) is particularly compelling.\n61\t- **Related work is thorough.** Six organized paradigms with ~42 references: grammar-constrained decoding, semantic constrained decoding for code, vocabulary/type-constrained generation, structural constraints via training, post-hoc ontology validation, and quality impact of constrained decoding.\n62\t- **PICARD differentiation well-articulated.** Clean technical distinction: PICARD operates over fixed, fully enumerated database schemas, while OntoCoDec's entity registry is populated during generation from free-text spans.\n63\t- **Honest positioning.** Title change from \"Guaranteed\" to \"Near-Complete,\" proactive CF disclosure, dual-factor model more insightful than simple tightness-only narrative.\n64\t- **Cross-model validation meaningful.** The RV–DR orthogonality (Llama: low RV but high DR; Qwen: opposite) justifies the two-layer design from a model-agnostic perspective.\n65\t\n66\t**Weaknesses:**\n67\t- **(Major) Core technical contribution has limited novelty.** Extending grammar-constrained decoding with a stateful semantic layer is a domain transfer of techniques from code generation (PICARD EMNLP'21, Synchromesh ICLR'22, MGD NeurIPS'23, PLDI'25 type systems → prefix automata) to a structurally *simpler* constraint system. IE has flat domain/range sets over small entity type inventories (6 types) vs. deep subtyping and parametric polymorphism in PL. The entity registry is a dictionary lookup, not a novel inference mechanism. Composition via bitwise AND is the standard approach.\n68\t- **(Major) Decidability taxonomy limited conceptual depth.** The local / prefix-decidable / non-local classification follows directly from well-known properties of autoregressive left-to-right generation. Similar taxonomies appear implicitly in Synchromesh and MGD. The paper would benefit from either rigorous formalization (connecting to formal language theory) or honest positioning as organizational framework rather than standalone contribution.\n69\t- **(Moderate) CRANE cited but not empirically compared.** CRANE occupies the space between post-hoc filtering and single-pass constrained decoding. The paper notes S2's two-pass strategy is \"analogous to CRANE's alternating strategy\" but provides no comparison.\n70\t- **(Moderate) Missing connections to neuro-symbolic and structured prediction literature.** DeepProbLog, Semantic Loss, posterior regularization, constraint relaxation literature — OntoCoDec's approach (compiling declarative constraints into procedural enforcement) is a specific instance of this broader research direction.\n71\t- **(Moderate) \"Enabling dependency\" inflated.** That grammar must validate relation names before semantic can look up rules is an architectural consequence of the design, not a deep insight about constraint composition.\n72\t- **(Minor) \"First systematic study\" should be scoped** to \"first systematic study of ontology constraint violations in zero-shot LLM-based IE.\"\n73\t- **(Minor) Missing citations:** TANL (Paolini et al., ICLR 2021), REBEL (Cabot & Navigli, EMNLP 2021), SynCode (Ugare et al., 2024).\n74\t\n75\t**Key Questions:**\n76\t1. What distinguishes OntoCoDec from PLDI'25 applied to a simpler constraint structure? What IE-specific technical challenge required a novel solution?\n77\t2. How does OntoCoDec differ from EnriCo (Zaratiana et al., 2024), which also enforces type constraints at generation time using ASP?\n78\t3. What happens when the model generates an entity in a relation not listed in the entity list? How does the registry handle missing entries?\n79\t4. Do violation landscape findings generalize beyond zero-shot? If fine-tuned models achieve low baseline DR, the practical motivation narrows substantially.\n80\t5. How sensitive is the tightness metric to using observed type pairs vs. the Cartesian product of dom(r) × range(r)?\n81\t\n82\t---\n83\t\n84\t## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n85\t\n86\t### R1's Response to R2\n87\t\n88\t**Agreements:**\n89\t- R2's framing of OntoCoDec as \"domain transfer to structurally simpler constraints\" is sharper than R1's original concern. R1 concedes this is the paper's most vulnerable flank.\n90\t- CRANE empirical comparison gap aligns with R1's missing-baselines concern.\n91\t- \"First systematic study\" should be scoped.\n92\t- Q4 (generalization beyond zero-shot) strengthens R1's ecological validity concern.\n93\t\n94\t**Disagreements:**\n95\t- R1 defends the **enabling dependency** as having empirical value: the 24% vs. 94% gap is non-trivially large, even if architecturally predictable. The quantitative magnitude is the contribution.\n96\t- R1 considers R2's **Excitement=2 too harsh**. The violation landscape study is a genuine first, 94% DR reduction at zero F1 cost is practically useful, and the system works with honest limitations reported.\n97\t\n98\t**Score revision:** R1 maintains Soundness=4, Excitement=3, Overall=3, Confidence=4.\n99\t\n100\t### R2's Response to R1\n101\t\n102\t**Agreements:**\n103\t- R1's narrow-scope concern reinforces R2's novelty concern from the experimental side.\n104\t- 8% R-F1 ecological validity aligns with R2's unstated practical deployment concern.\n105\t- DocRED 10.21% residual DR matches R2's \"near-complete\" skepticism.\n106\t- CF severity distinction unvalidated — shared concern.\n107\t\n108\t**Disagreements:**\n109\t- R2 finds R1 **too generous on the ablation**: the enabling dependency is architecturally self-evident rather than a discovery.\n110\t- R2 finds R1 **too lenient on statistical rigor**: DR/CF metrics are single-run point estimates with no CIs. The \"94% DR reduction\" headline has no confidence interval.\n111\t\n112\t**New insights from R1:**\n113\t- R1's Q1 (fallback-rate/residual-DR tautology) is sharp — if the match is architectural, the \"tightness predicts DR\" claim is partially circular.\n114\t- Llama RV-vs-DR orthogonality partially addresses R2's novelty concern on the empirical side.\n115\t\n116\t**Score revision:** R2 would move Excitement to 2.5 if half-points permitted; holds at 2 on integer scale. Maintains Soundness=4, Overall=3.\n117\t\n118\t---\n119\t\n120\t## Step 3: Convergence\n121\t\n122\t### Agreed Points (Both Reviewers)\n123\t\n124\t| Dimension | Consensus |\n125\t|-----------|-----------|\n126\t| **Soundness** | 4 (Strong). Experimental design is rigorous: ablation cleanly isolates layers, statistical analysis is proper, negative results honestly reported. |\n127\t| **Overall** | 3 (Borderline). Technically solid but bounded contributions. |\n128\t| **Violation landscape** | Genuine empirical contribution; first systematic characterization of per-relation DR distributions in LLM-based IE. |\n129\t| **Ablation quality** | Well-designed 4-way ablation on two datasets with cross-model validation. |\n130\t| **Honest reporting** | CF trade-off, grammar DR increase on DocRED, S2 null result, post-hoc F1 advantage — all transparently disclosed. |\n131\t| **CF unvalidated** | DR \"hard\" / CF \"soft\" severity distinction asserted but not empirically confirmed via downstream evaluation. |\n132\t| **Scope narrow** | 2 models × 2 datasets × zero-shot only insufficient for \"model-agnostic\" / broad IE claims. |\n133\t| **\"Near-complete\" strained** | 10.21% residual DR on Qwen-DocRED contradicts title framing. |\n134\t| **CRANE comparison missing** | Cited but not empirically compared; notable gap. |\n135\t| **Decidability taxonomy** | Limited novelty; useful as organizational framework, not as standalone theoretical contribution. |\n136\t\n137\t### Disagreed Points\n138\t\n139\t| Dimension | R1 | R2 | Resolution |\n140\t|-----------|-----|-----|-----------|\n141\t| **Excitement** | 3 (Borderline) | 2 (Mediocre) | Split. R1 weights practical utility; R2 weights novelty gap vs. code-gen literature. **Consensus: 2.5 → round to 3** with explicit caveat that novelty is the primary risk factor. |\n142\t| **Enabling dependency** | Empirically validated (24% vs 94% gap is non-trivial) | Architecturally self-evident | **Consensus:** The quantitative gap adds modest value but should not be listed as a standalone contribution. |\n143\t| **Statistical rigor** | Praised (bootstrap, McNemar) | Insufficient (DR/CF lack CIs) | **Consensus:** F1 analysis is statistically rigorous; violation rate metrics lack uncertainty quantification. Mixed verdict. |\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n148\t\n149\t### Final Scores\n150\t\n151\t| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n152\t|-----------|-------|---------------|\n153\t| **Soundness** | **4** (Strong) | Experimental design is thorough: 4-way ablation isolating each layer, 2×2 cross-model matrix, 10,000-iteration bootstrap for F1, McNemar tests for prompt effects, post-hoc multi-dimensional comparison. Claims are carefully calibrated to evidence. Negative results honestly reported. Gap: violation rate metrics lack uncertainty quantification; no downstream CF impact evaluation. |\n154\t| **Excitement** | **3** (Borderline) | The violation landscape study is a genuine empirical first. The system achieves strong DR reduction (94% on SciERC) at zero F1 cost with an honest CF trade-off analysis. The dual-factor model and RV–DR orthogonality are real analytical contributions. However, the core technical mechanism (stateful logit masking for domain/range constraints) is domain transfer of techniques well-established in code generation (PICARD, Synchromesh, PLDI'25) to a structurally simpler constraint system. The decidability taxonomy restates known properties of autoregressive generation. Novelty is the primary risk factor. |\n155\t| **Overall** | **3** (Borderline) | A well-executed systems paper with clean experiments and honest reporting, filling a genuine gap (no prior work compiles IE ontology domain/range constraints into decoding-time masks). The violation landscape study, thorough ablation, cross-model validation, and dual-factor analysis are all positives. However: (1) limited novelty of the core framework — competent domain transfer, not new algorithms; (2) narrow scope (2×2, zero-shot only, ~8% F1); (3) substantial CF trade-off without downstream validation; (4) 10% residual DR on DocRED strains \"near-complete\" framing. Above the bar for **Findings**; borderline for main conference. |\n156\t| **Confidence** | **4** (Quite sure) | Both reviewers are familiar with constrained decoding, IE methods, and the code-generation constraint literature. |\n157\t\n158\t### Recommendation\n159\t\n160\t**Borderline — lean toward Findings track rather than main conference.**\n161\t\n162\tThe paper makes a genuine empirical contribution (violation landscape study, tightness-driven analysis) and demonstrates a working system with clean experiments. The main weakness is that the technical framework itself — compiling ontology constraints into grammar + semantic decoding masks — is a natural and relatively straightforward adaptation of techniques already established in code generation to a simpler constraint domain. EMNLP main track typically expects either a more novel technical contribution or a substantially broader empirical study.\n163\t\n164\t### Key Revisions That Would Strengthen the Paper\n165\t\n166\t1. **Add a fine-tuned or few-shot baseline** to establish whether the violation landscape and constraint enforcement benefits hold in a higher-accuracy regime. This is the single most impactful addition.\n167\t2. **Downstream CF impact evaluation.** Run the constrained vs. unconstrained output through a downstream task (e.g., question answering over the extracted KG) to empirically validate the DR-hard/CF-soft severity distinction.\n168\t3. **CRANE empirical comparison.** Compare against constrained re-generation, not just post-hoc filtering.\n169\t4. **Add CIs for violation rate metrics.** The headline \"94% DR reduction\" currently has no confidence interval.\n170\t5. **Scope claims more carefully.** \"Near-complete\" is justified on SciERC (0.93%) and Llama-DocRED (2.55%) but not on Qwen-DocRED (10.21%). The title and abstract should reflect this variance.\n171\t6. **Sharpen novelty positioning vs. code-gen literature.** A dedicated paragraph explaining what IE-specific challenge required a solution beyond what PICARD/Synchromesh/PLDI'25 already provide.\n172\t\n173\t### Experiment Coverage Assessment\n174\t\n175\tAll planned experiments from the design document are reflected in the paper:\n176\t- **Phase 0 (violation landscape):** P001–P004 ✓ (Tables 1–3, §3)\n177\t- **Phase 2 (ablation):** P005a, P005–P008 (SciERC), P009–P012 (DocRED) ✓ (Tables 4–7, §5.2–5.4)\n178\t- **Cross-model:** P013–P016 ✓ (Table 8–9, §5.5)\n179\t- **Post-hoc baseline:** P018 ✓ (Table 10, §5.6)\n180\t- **Bootstrap CI:** P019 ✓ (Table 11, §5.7)\n181\t- **S2 pilot (P017):** Correctly omitted (engineering checkpoint)\n182\t- **P020:** Correctly omitted (killed)\n183\t\n184\tCoverage is complete. The paper reflects all non-killed experiments from the design.\n185\t\n186\t### Summary\n187\t\n188\tOntoCoDec is a technically competent paper with above-average experimental rigor and commendable honesty about its limitations. The violation landscape study and the dual-factor analysis are the strongest intellectual contributions. The framework itself works as described but represents domain adaptation of known constrained-decoding techniques rather than algorithmic innovation. The 2×2 zero-shot evaluation, while internally thorough, leaves open the question of practical utility in the regime where IE systems are actually deployed (fine-tuned, higher accuracy). The CF trade-off, while honestly reported, lacks downstream validation of the asserted severity distinction. These factors place the paper at the borderline for EMNLP main conference, with a clear path to strengthening through broader evaluation and sharper novelty positioning.\n189\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012cpiPbcALRrGoQNXCn6Urx","session_id":"c22f8857-e04e-4f5b-a2f0-8fc78112cf68","uuid":"b1f38eee-3864-4ddd-882b-e07ecc2f7dad","timestamp":"2026-05-14T21:36:54.697Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"ae512c7d027e95370","tool_use_id":"toolu_01F3Xghc4RURwN6jn2H1NNNC","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":83672,"tool_uses":29,"duration_ms":164812},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"6a928dc6-0260-4918-86af-59cee1f2e38f","session_id":"c22f8857-e04e-4f5b-a2f0-8fc78112cf68"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HP15mjGSVb44RFhuFB1iCZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PfncNWTAgJYM4jkDxgVhcE","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"schema_semantic_constrained_ie","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T205155","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"## Reviewer 1 (R1) — Methodology Soundness & Experimental Rigor\n\n## Summary of the Paper\n\nThis paper presents OntoCoDec, a two-layer constrained decoding framework for LLM-based information extraction that compiles ontology schemas into token-level masks. A grammar layer (XGrammar CFG) enforces JSON structure and entity/relation vocabulary membership, while a semantic layer maintains a stateful entity registry and applies dynamic logit masking to enforce domain/range constraints. The paper also contributes a systematic violation landscape study showing that 15-19% of zero-shot IE triples violate ontology constraints, driven by constraint tightness rather than ontology scale. Evaluated on SciERC and DocRED with Qwen3-8B and Llama-3.1-8B, OntoCoDec achieves 94% DR reduction on SciERC and 45-90% on DocRED at zero or positive F1 cost, but trades DR violations for elevated cross-field consistency (CF) violations (up to 9.9-12%).\n\n## Summary of Strengths\n\n- **S1: Well-motivated two-layer architecture with clear decidability taxonomy.** The decomposition of ontology constraints into local (grammar-expressible) vs. prefix-decidable (requiring state) vs. non-local (out of scope) categories is principled and clearly articulated. The \"enabling dependency\" argument—that grammar is a prerequisite for semantic, not merely additive—is convincingly supported by ablation: semantic-only achieves 24% DR reduction vs. 94% with both layers. The grammar-only row in Table 5 (DocRED) where DR *increases* from 18.46% to 26.17% is a particularly revealing result that the authors explain well (latent violations surfaced when OOV relations are forced into valid vocabulary), and it directly motivates the semantic layer.\n\n- **S2: Thorough ablation design and honest reporting.** The five-way SciERC ablation and four-way DocRED ablation are well-constructed. Each layer is tested independently and in combination, providing clean causal evidence for the enabling dependency claim. The paper is unusually transparent about negative findings: CF violations increase substantially (0.08% to 9.90%), S2 does not solve CF, DocRED DR reduction is weaker than SciERC, Llama Entity F1 declines slightly on DocRED, and parse failures increase. This proactive disclosure substantially strengthens credibility.\n\n- **S3: The violation landscape study is a genuine empirical contribution.** The finding that violation rates are driven by constraint tightness rather than ontology scale (SciERC 15.4% with 7 relations vs. DocRED 18.7% with 96 relations; per-relation rates spanning 0.76% to 42.4%) is non-obvious and useful to the community independent of the proposed method. The schema-informed prompting comparison adds value, especially the Evaluate-for regression (+5.8pp) which concretely demonstrates prior-constraint conflict.\n\n- **S4: Cross-model validation and statistical rigor.** The 2x2 matrix (2 models, 2 datasets) with bootstrap CIs is appropriate for the claims made. The paper correctly reports non-significance on SciERC (p=0.58) and significance on DocRED (p<0.001) with mechanistic attribution (OOV elimination). The dual-factor analysis (tightness x model RV baseline) to explain the Qwen vs. Llama DocRED gap is a creditable analytical finding.\n\n- **S5: Post-hoc comparison is fair and multi-dimensional.** The comparison does not cherry-pick metrics—it honestly reports that post-hoc achieves higher F1 (SciERC 8.63 vs. 8.36; DocRED 9.61 vs. 8.23) while noting the recall loss (127 TPs lost on DocRED, 48.1% triple rejection) and residual ET violations that post-hoc cannot address. The framing as complementary approaches rather than competing ones is appropriate.\n\n## Summary of Weaknesses\n\n- **W1 [Major]: The CF trade-off is quantitatively severe and inadequately resolved.** The method trades DR violations (15.37% → 0.93%) for CF violations (0.08% → 9.90%) on SciERC, and CF reaches 12.02% for Llama on DocRED. While the paper argues CF is \"softer\" than DR (each triple remains individually type-valid), the practical severity depends on the downstream application. If a knowledge graph consumer expects entity type consistency—which is a reasonable expectation—then CF violations are errors, not mere preferences. The paper correctly attributes CF to per-relation independent enforcement and identifies the Generic↔OtherScientificTerm ambiguity pattern, but the argument that this reflects \"genuine semantic ambiguity\" rather than method failure is only partially convincing. Some CF (e.g., MISC↔PER) would clearly be method-induced errors, not type system ambiguity. The paper needs: (a) a breakdown of CF into \"ambiguity-driven\" vs. \"unambiguously erroneous\" categories with proportions, and (b) a net violation rate analysis (total violations before vs. after constraining) to show the overall constraint profile improves even accounting for CF.\n\n- **W2 [Major]: DocRED DR reduction (45% for Qwen) is weak for a system claiming \"near-complete type-valid IE.\"** The title promises \"near-complete\" but 10.21% residual DR on DocRED (Qwen) is not near-complete by any reasonable interpretation. The constraint coverage / tightness explanation is necessary but insufficient: if the method systematically fails on tight-constraint ontologies, and real-world ontologies (Wikidata, UMLS, FIBO) are typically tight, then the practical applicability claim is weakened. The paper should: (a) report what fraction of real-world ontologies have tightness profiles similar to SciERC vs. DocRED, and (b) discuss whether the grammar-first fallback strategy could be improved (e.g., by relaxing to the k nearest valid type rather than full grammar fallback). The Llama result (90.2% reduction on DocRED) partially mitigates this concern but raises the question: if the framework can achieve 90% on the same ontology with a different model, the residual is model-dependent, not ontology-fundamental. This undermines the tightness-as-bottleneck narrative.\n\n- **W3 [Minor]: Low absolute F1 (~8%) makes it difficult to assess practical impact.** The authors correctly frame F1 as a control variable and cite supervised SOTA (49-53%), but the concern is not about SOTA comparison—it's about whether the violation patterns observed at 8% F1 generalize to the 30-50% F1 regime where the method would actually be deployed. At 8% F1, the model is generating mostly incorrect triples; the violation patterns on these incorrect triples may differ substantially from violations on partially-correct outputs from a fine-tuned model. A brief experiment with a fine-tuned or few-shot model would substantially strengthen the paper's practical relevance claim. The paper asserts \"when applied to fine-tuned models... the same constraints remain necessary\" but provides zero evidence for this claim.\n\n- **W4 [Minor]: The decidability taxonomy, while useful, overstates its theoretical contribution.** The distinction between local, prefix-decidable, and non-local constraints is reasonable but is essentially a restatement of the context-free vs. context-sensitive distinction from formal language theory, applied to this specific domain. The paper presents this as a novel taxonomy (Contribution 2 framing), but it would be more honest to present it as an application of well-known formal language concepts to the IE constraint domain. The taxonomy also has a gap: the paper does not discuss constraints that are prefix-decidable in principle but computationally intractable to check at each decoding step—are there such constraints in larger ontologies?\n\n- **W5 [Minor]: Related work is comprehensive but the novelty boundary with PICARD and PLDI'25 type-constrained code generation is thin.** The paper acknowledges the mechanistic similarity with PLDI'25 (\"same type system → automaton → logits mask pipeline\") and differentiates on domain (code vs. IE) and the stateful entity registry. However, the entity registry is a relatively straightforward engineering contribution—it tracks (name, type) pairs as they are generated. The core algorithmic idea (compile type constraints into logit masks) exists in prior work. The novelty is primarily in the *application domain* and the *violation landscape study*, which the paper should foreground more explicitly rather than claiming a novel framework contribution.\n\n- **W6 [Minor]: Only zero-shot evaluation.** The paper evaluates exclusively in the zero-shot setting. While this isolates the constraint enforcement effect, it also means the method is validated only in a regime where it is least likely to be practically deployed. At minimum, a few-shot evaluation (3-5 examples) would show whether the method's benefits persist as baseline quality improves.\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1**: Can you provide a net violation rate (DR + RV + ET + CF combined, weighted or unweighted) before and after OntoCoDec, to give a single summary metric of whether the overall ontology compliance improves or merely shifts violation types? On SciERC: unconstrained total ≈ 17.24% (DR+RV+ET+CF), full S1 total ≈ 10.83% (DR+RV+ET+CF). This would mean the *total* violation burden decreased by only 37%, not 94%. Is this a fair characterization?\n\n- **Q2**: The paper shows that Llama achieves 90.2% DR reduction on DocRED while Qwen achieves only 45% on the same ontology, and attributes this to Qwen's higher baseline RV. But this means the *same* constraint framework's effectiveness varies by 2x depending on the model, which contradicts the \"model-agnostic\" framing. How should a practitioner decide whether OntoCoDec will be effective for their model+ontology combination without running the full experiment?\n\n- **Q3**: The graceful degradation (grammar-first fallback) is triggered when S(t) ∩ G(t) = ∅. What happens to the probability distribution in these cases? The model is forced to generate a grammar-valid but semantically invalid token. Does this cause cascading errors in subsequent tokens, or is the effect localized? The near-perfect correlation between fallback rate and residual DR (10.2% vs. 10.21%) suggests each fallback produces exactly one violation, but is this verified?\n\n- **Q4**: The first-token masking implementation assumes each entity type maps to a unique first subword token. This works for SciERC (6 types) and DocRED (6 types), but would it hold for ontologies with 50+ entity types? What is the scalability limit of this design choice?\n\n- **Q5**: Have you measured the wall-clock overhead of OntoCoDec (grammar + semantic layer) compared to unconstrained decoding? The paper mentions \"marginal overhead from logits masking\" but provides no timing data.\n\n## Missing References\n\n- Josifoski et al. (2022), \"GenIE: Generative Information Extraction\" — appears in the extended related work but should be cited in the main paper as it is a direct precursor for constrained autoregressive IE.\n- Zaratiana et al. (2024), \"EnriCo\" — uses ASP constraints during joint entity-relation decoding; appears only in the appendix related work section but is highly relevant to the main related work discussion.\n- The paper could benefit from citing work on constrained decoding distribution distortion (Park et al. 2024, \"Grammar-Aligned Decoding\") in the context of whether logit masking distorts the model's output distribution, which is directly relevant to the CF trade-off discussion.\n\n## Detailed Comments\n\n**Section 2 (Background):** The formalization is clean. The constraint tightness definition τ(r) = 1 - |σ(r)|/|E|^2 is well-chosen and the connection to violation likelihood is empirically supported. One concern: the definition uses |E|^2 as the denominator, treating head and tail type spaces as independent. But domain and range are defined separately (dom(r) and range(r)), so the actual constraint space is |dom(r)| × |range(r)| out of |E| × |E|. The tightness formula is correct but could be more explicitly connected to the dom/range decomposition in the text.\n\n**Section 3 (Violation Landscape):** The violation landscape is the paper's most compelling independent contribution. The per-relation analysis on SciERC is informative. However, the DocRED landscape analysis is surprisingly shallow—only aggregate numbers are reported, with no per-relation breakdown (the paper mentions P31 instance_of at 60.4% in passing but no systematic analysis). Given that DocRED has 96 relations and the tightness argument is central, a per-relation scatter plot of tightness vs. DR for DocRED would significantly strengthen the paper.\n\n**Section 4 (Method):** The method is clearly described. The position detection mechanism (pattern matching on decoded prefix to identify active field) is under-specified—how robust is this to unexpected token sequences? If the grammar layer is active, unexpected sequences should be impossible, but during semantic-only decoding the paper reports ET violations *increasing* (4.22% vs. 2.48% on DocRED), which suggests the semantic layer can perturb the generation distribution in ways that break the position detector's assumptions. More detail on failure modes would be helpful.\n\n**Section 5 (Experiments):** The experimental design is solid. Key observations:\n\n1. The SciERC ablation table is well-structured and the narrative clearly explains each row's contribution. The enabling dependency argument (semantic-only: 24% DR reduction; full S1: 94%) is the paper's strongest empirical result.\n\n2. The DocRED grammar-only DR *increase* (18.46% → 26.17%) is a fascinating finding that is well-explained. This is one of the most interesting results in the paper and could be highlighted more prominently.\n\n3. The post-hoc comparison is fair. The 48.1% rejection rate on DocRED is a striking number that clearly illustrates the practical limitation of post-hoc approaches.\n\n4. The cross-model section is adequate but would benefit from a more systematic analysis. Currently it reads as \"Llama also works\" rather than revealing model-specific patterns. The RV baseline → latent DR mechanism is interesting but buried.\n\n5. The bootstrap CI analysis is well-executed. Reporting per-dataset significance rather than a pooled metric is the correct methodological choice.\n\n**Section 6 (Related Work):** The extended version (appendix) is thorough with 42 references. The main-text version covers the key paradigms adequately. The PICARD distinction (fixed schema vs. dynamically populated entity registry) is the strongest differentiator and could be made more prominent.\n\n**Section 7 (Discussion/Conclusion):** The CF severity argument is reasonable but needs the quantitative support requested in W1. The \"F1 as control variable\" framing is appropriate. The future work directions are concrete and relevant.\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The paper makes a genuine contribution in two areas: (1) the violation landscape study, which is the first systematic measurement of ontology constraint violations in LLM-based IE and provides useful empirical findings (tightness-driven violations, prompt insufficiency scaling), and (2) a working system that substantially reduces type violations at decoding time. The main concerns are the CF trade-off severity, the weak DocRED results for Qwen, and the narrow evaluation setting (zero-shot only, 2 models, 2 datasets). The paper is well-written, transparent about limitations, and provides clean ablation evidence. It falls in the borderline accept range—strong enough for a Findings paper, and potentially for the main conference if the CF analysis and DocRED narrative are strengthened in revision.\n\n## Scores\n- Soundness: 3.5/5\n- Excitement: 3/5\n- Overall: 3.5/5\n- Confidence: 4/5\n","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":240,"cache_read_input_tokens":82522,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":240,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F3Xghc4RURwN6jn2H1NNNC","session_id":"c22f8857-e04e-4f5b-a2f0-8fc78112cf68","uuid":"4c1a44ec-c03e-488c-a5cb-abc3aa450143"}
